# Windows Filesystem Navigation & Management

## 1. Navigation

| Command      | Description                                                          |
| ------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `cd <path>`  | Change the current directory                                         |
| `cd ..`      | Move to the parent directory                                         |
| `cd \`       | Move to the root of the current drive                                |
| `ls` / `dir` | List files and directories (`Get-ChildItem` in PowerShell)           |
| `pwd`        | Display the current working directory (`Get-Location` in PowerShell) |
| `tree`       | Display the directory structure as a tree                            |

## 2. Creating Files & Directories

| Command                | Description                                             |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| `mkdir <name>`         | Create a directory                                      |
| `type nul > file.txt`  | Create an empty file (CMD)                              |
| `type file.txt`        | Display file contents in CMD; similar to `cat` on Linux |
| `Get-Content file.txt` | Display file contents in PowerShell                     |

## 3. Deleting Files & Directories

| Command                                 | Description                                                        |
| --------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `del file.txt`                          | Delete a file                                                      |
| `rmdir directory`                       | Delete an empty directory                                          |
| `rmdir /s /q directory`                 | Delete a directory and all its contents without confirmation (CMD) |
| `Remove-Item directory -Recurse -Force` | Delete a directory and its contents (PowerShell)                   |

> [!warning] Be careful
> Recursive deletion can permanently remove files and subdirectories. Verify the target path before executing the command.

## 4. Renaming

| Command                       | Description                              |
| ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| `ren old.txt new.txt`         | Rename a file or directory in CMD        |
| `Rename-Item old.txt new.txt` | Rename a file or directory in PowerShell |

## 5. Linux vs. Windows Command Equivalents

| Linux                         | CMD                      | PowerShell                    |
| ----------------------------- | ------------------------ | ----------------------------- |
| `ls`                          | `dir`                    | `Get-ChildItem`               |
| `pwd`                         | `cd` (without arguments) | `Get-Location`                |
| `cat file.txt`                | `type file.txt`          | `Get-Content file.txt`        |
| `mkdir test`                  | `mkdir test`             | `mkdir test`                  |
| `rm file.txt`                 | `del file.txt`           | `Remove-Item file.txt`        |
| `mv old.txt new.txt` (rename) | `ren old.txt new.txt`    | `Rename-Item old.txt new.txt` |
| `cd ..`                       | `cd ..`                  | `cd ..`                       |
| `tree`                        | `tree`                   | `tree`                        |

> [!note] Important distinction
> PowerShell supports many familiar aliases, including `ls`, `dir`, `pwd`, `cat`, and `rm`. These map to PowerShell cmdlets such as `Get-ChildItem`, `Get-Location`, `Get-Content`, and `Remove-Item`. They are aliases, not the original Linux commands.
